<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hatay Province</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hatay is the southernmost province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, with its capital at Antakya, ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>. As the Sanjak of Alexandretta it was part of French-mandated Syria until it became the autonomous Hatay State in 1938 and was annexed by Turkey in 1939, a transfer Syria long contested. It was severely damaged by the earthquakes of February 2023.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hatay-province.jpg" alt="Hatay Province" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zaviye Film · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Payas_Castle,_Hatay,_Turkey.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hatay lies mostly outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, along the eastern coast of the Levantine Sea, and borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> to the south and east. Its second-largest city is İskenderun, formerly Alexandretta. About 46 per cent of the land is mountain, 33 per cent plain and 20 per cent plateau and hillside. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orontes-river">Orontes River</a> runs through it and reaches the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> at a delta in Samandağ.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has been settled since the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. It belonged in turn to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>, the Amorite kingdom of Yamhad and the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>, and was then ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> and Neo-Hittite peoples, from whom the modern name derives. It was the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, home to the four Greek cities of the Syrian tetrapolis, and from 64 BCE Antioch was an important centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> conquered the region in 638, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> took it in 1098 and made it the centre of the Principality of Antioch. The Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> took it from the Mameluks in 1516.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> the area was the sanjak of Alexandretta. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> it was placed under the French mandate for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. A French-Turkish treaty of 20 October 1921 made the sanjak autonomous, and it then had 220,000 inhabitants, 87,000 of them Turks, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>. In 1936 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> complained to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> about the treatment of the Turkish community, and the sanjak was given autonomy in November 1937.</p><p>Hatay State, a transitional entity, existed from 7 September 1938 to 29 June 1939. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was willing to return the area to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> in exchange for a pact against possible European aggression. On 29 June 1939, after a referendum that has been called rigged, Hatay became a Turkish province. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> disputed both the separation and the annexation, and about 50,000 people, among them 22,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, are estimated to have left for Syria.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Hatay is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>'s southernmost province, with its capital at Antakya, the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, and a population of 1,686,043 in 2022. Its people include Sunni Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alawites">Alawites</a>, with Syriac Orthodox, Maronite, Antiochian Greek and Armenian communities. Its sites include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalakh">Alalakh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-tayinat">Tell Tayinat</a> and the Hatay Archaeology Museum at Antakya, with a large collection of Roman mosaics. The province was heavily damaged by the earthquakes of 2023.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hatay Province” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After elections held under Turkish military supervision gave Turks a majority, the assembly of the Sanjak of Alexandretta proclaimed the State of <b>Hatay</b> in September 1938, with Antakya (Antioch) as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/hatay-state">Hatay State</a></li></ul></section>
